{"number": 51, "text": " 71          Eye,       my,        mine\n         |   Introducing            syllables\n  A         We  can divide A word into one or more  syllables. For example mum  has one syllable, mother has\n            two  syllables and grandmother has three syllables. A syllable is a group of one or more sounds.\n                                           Lan                 A         ic A      af ane ar mare\n            The  essential part of a syllable is a vowel sound (V). Some syllables are just one vowel sound.\n            For example,  these words have one  syllable, and the syllable is just one vowel sound:\n            eye /ai/, owe /av/.\n            A  syllable can have consonant sounds (C) before the V, after the V or before and after the V.\n            Here  are some more  examples  (they are all words of one syllable).\n            —\n              CV                             vc\n              go                             if                              ten /ten/\n              my /mat/                       egg /eg/                        nose\n              know /nou/                     ice                             mouth  /mau6/\n              weigh                          eight /eit/                     knife /naif/\n       A     Note: There may be more than one C before or after the V. (See Units 24, 25.)\n             Remember   that letters are mot the same as sounds. For example, the consonant letters W and Y\n             are not consonant sounds  if they come after the vowel sound in the syllable e.g. saw, say. They\n             are part of the vowel sound. In some accents, for example South East English, the same is true\n             for the consonant letter R. Here are some more examples. They  are words  of one syllable and\n             they all have the pattern CV.\n             how         law /lo:/  pay        why        car            /heo/\n             Some  people use the word  syllable to talk about the parts of a written word. But in this book,\n             the word  syllable is used to talk about the pronunciation of words, not the writing. For example,\n             in writing we can divide ‘chocolate’ into three parts like this: cho-co-late. But when we say the\n             word,  we pronounce  only two  syllables, like this: chocolate        (The dot shows where  the\n             two  syllables are divided.) A number of other words may be pronounced   with fewer syllables\n             than  in writing. Listen to these examples.\n         B1_ chocolate                different              interesting /‘in.tras.t1n/\n              general                           /‘kamf.ta.bol/              /‘sek.ra.tri/\n              The first syllable in these words has the same three sounds, but in the opposite order:\n              kitchen           chicken\n              If a sentence has similar-sounding syllables like this in it, it may be difficult to say.\n              These sentences are called ‘tongue-twisters’. Listen to this example.\n\n              Richard checked  the chicken in the kitchen.\n\n\n         A    Note: You can find more about syllables in Units 24 to 27.\n\n                 English Pronunciation in Use", "char_count": 3184, "mean_conf": 86.69, "word_count": 450, "words": [{"text": "two", "x0": 243, "y0": 378, "x1": 316, "y1": 403, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "syllables", "x0": 333, "y0": 365, "x1": 490, "y1": 412, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "and", "x0": 507, "y0": 364, "x1": 576, "y1": 401, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "grandmother", "x0": 590, "y0": 362, "x1": 843, "y1": 410, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "has", "x0": 858, "y0": 361, "x1": 919, "y1": 398, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "three", "x0": 935, "y0": 360, "x1": 1029, "y1": 398, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "syllables.", "x0": 1046, "y0": 359, "x1": 1214, "y1": 407, "conf": 94, "bold": false}, {"text": "A", "x0": 1231, "y0": 360, 